# Check every text file out with LF, on every machine.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set on the Windows build machine, so git stored LF and
# handed the working tree CRLF. That is invisible until something reads a source
# file as text -- and this repo does that a lot. The `*.sync.test.js` contract
# tests read migrations and config and assert on their content, and several match
# across a line break:
#
#     sql.indexOf('agents_agent_role_valid\n  check')
#
# Against a CRLF working tree that is `\r\n  check` and the search returns -1, so
# the test reports the constraint as missing when it is sitting right there. Five
# tests failed that way the first time the repo was checked out fresh on Windows,
# in three files, all of them contract tests and none of them actually broken.
#
# Pinning `eol=lf` costs nothing: git already stores LF, so this changes what
# lands in the working tree, not what is committed. Every editor in normal use
# handles LF, and the alternative -- teaching each test to tolerate both endings
# -- would have to be remembered by every test written from here on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch. `text=auto` detects most of these on its own;
# saying so explicitly means a corrupt file can never be blamed on detection.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.webp    binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.woff    binary
*.woff2   binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
*.mp4     binary
*.keystore binary
